#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/id #uuid "f7324b66-d8f7-5bdb-9786-4160808f2a7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4768" :question/problem "Given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/assets/d4fa85cab74f4bda57059d1c6abc9dacea665531ce84ba9d2f1598a13c49ef60.png) \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "58955d86-8a94-5174-83fa-072314037298" :question/math-academy-id "e-4769" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $F$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1, \\qquad\n\tR_3:=R_3+2R_1\n$$\n\nGiven that $F$ has the LU factorization $F=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying $R_2:=R_2 + {\\color{red}1} \\cdot R_1$ and then $R_3:=R_3+ {\\color{red}2} \\cdot R_1$ is equivalent to multiplying $F$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/assets/5eb696ca679a0079e8460d472b6f172df4f2e29f5dfef6cbdee388c55dcb1db5.png)\n\nNow, since $E_2E_1F=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}F & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}F & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ F & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ F & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ F & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/id #uuid "50685ac4-9980-5841-a669-783f6086a9dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4770" :question/problem "Given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14062432/assets/e85dac75798b6cb463403f54ab2a675206d7eeea0a8958081aac9c696047b345.png)\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_2E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_2$ and $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5}{4} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b25255-0e2d-55ac-8710-1f57a35ec1c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343419} {:answer/id #uuid "7592a942-792a-5caa-9389-c03d94a7e772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343420} {:answer/id #uuid "28bfd293-a1a8-52a3-a5e5-9ffd2ca435a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343421} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b9df09-ac34-5091-b157-310737b8e94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343422} {:answer/id #uuid "b35d40ce-8638-5109-b727-607b6c6fe7b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7592a942-792a-5caa-9389-c03d94a7e772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343420} :answer-field/id #uuid "e907506d-8508-57fb-9ed9-667447ee24db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "103c5b53-bb22-5ad8-9493-a7215f314160" :question/math-academy-id "q-106836" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4d10f94-98d4-53d8-a35c-e65a06dd2c55" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using a Single Elementary Row Operation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f0830b6-da5a-52b8-a026-ad4d4d9d25ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343426} {:answer/id #uuid "030fd6cd-5bd3-5f95-b2e4-55310eb18f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343427} {:answer/id #uuid "3e418a10-8ea8-560e-b987-98500394f6d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343428} {:answer/id #uuid "287a7c90-3f3b-5d63-b67f-a06ff438c0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343429} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0b8c47-a4e4-56c3-9436-77fd09a67f26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "030fd6cd-5bd3-5f95-b2e4-55310eb18f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343427} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccadfa95-92b2-5274-ab0a-e1d590b3bf41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "682029cb-a24a-5772-887f-fb965b3c043f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106842" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f3bf2d-2972-50a0-b87d-a0486788898d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343433} {:answer/id #uuid "e3edbf67-788c-5d4c-b0f1-d647aadd3d76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343434} {:answer/id #uuid "9ba69622-7726-50fe-9e54-8b0bb00668bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343435} {:answer/id #uuid "2a79c620-eab7-5558-98a8-6e25801e6b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343436} {:answer/id #uuid "765a7a55-0f1a-5c94-b0d3-a3a228374c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "765a7a55-0f1a-5c94-b0d3-a3a228374c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343437} :answer-field/id #uuid "b145703d-d381-58b8-ac10-a60c3c9d106e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "032565e5-8acf-5267-a8b8-76412210c134" :question/math-academy-id "q-106843" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $C$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9{R}_{1}\n$$\n\nGiven that $C$ has the LU factorization $C=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+9\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $C$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot C=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}C=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}C & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}C & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ C & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ C & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ C & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343438 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c91fb2b-5607-555d-b897-f0e76765dfa6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Lower Triangular Matrix Given the Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cb72c1d-1ec2-5e52-8fdc-2e0ed3c7bb77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343440} {:answer/id #uuid "e0852b36-4ef0-5569-881e-d39b60374b08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343441} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5ab7bc-5e36-5982-b680-498fbb793b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343442} {:answer/id #uuid "4fabfc60-af43-5dfe-b22c-58b77d4fe0e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343443} {:answer/id #uuid "f18b9b49-23d2-5987-b705-4972e38aecc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f18b9b49-23d2-5987-b705-4972e38aecc2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343444} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dd1d7b8-9ae2-59f6-b652-0e2630d93e34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7278f295-d94d-5655-a394-3d2aeae2f280" :question/math-academy-id "q-106847" :question/problem "Suppose that a $3\\times 3$ matrix $H$ is reduced to row echelon form using the following elementary row operations, applied in the order in which they appear:\n\n$$\n{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1},\\,{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $H$ has the LU factorization $H=LU,$ then $L=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ and then ${R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{1}$ is equivalent to multiplying $H$ from the left by the following elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{E}_{1}}\\cdot H=U\n$$\n\nNow, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}H=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}H & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}H & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ H & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ H & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ H & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}\\cdot U\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521dc4b8-d1ed-5502-857b-32eeefac84b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343447} {:answer/id #uuid "8f3f02ca-4742-537a-bd53-246824b9ca65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343448} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b5b10c-bdf4-5fac-bfa2-16db04c6e00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343449} {:answer/id #uuid "ce039fa1-5192-5298-9f9e-ec2a91d29a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343450} {:answer/id #uuid "258b76d7-50f4-541c-a0eb-9fb8d16d80c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6b5b10c-bdf4-5fac-bfa2-16db04c6e00d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343449} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6046647-42d6-54bd-af8a-b8735dae4cd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b19ad2e-f600-587b-82e8-94b6f5ec8a03" :question/math-academy-id "q-106855" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -8 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{31}=4,{u}_{23}=2,$ and ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{23}=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cf03183-ae4f-53df-b93e-424cdfcb08e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343454} {:answer/id #uuid "0a5a7310-a166-5c9b-aa30-f75910cfd486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343455} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8272f3-61f1-5f10-a44a-ac71f0e9e29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343456} {:answer/id #uuid "6f30b0e3-10d5-54b5-a457-e4c15ceac2a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343457} {:answer/id #uuid "421be741-e9ce-5f30-af88-8a73de96b91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "421be741-e9ce-5f30-af88-8a73de96b91b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343458} :answer-field/id #uuid "3102dd81-817c-5c07-abda-1ff5d5a19e74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f435648-2d36-5e4d-b72c-5ba9f10c97f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-106856" :question/problem "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ has the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{2}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{3}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot {E}_{2}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{3},$ ${E}_{2},$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ {E}_{2}{E}_{1}A & ={E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ {E}_{1}A & ={E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}{E}_{3}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}])U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-2,$ ${u}_{33}=3,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e460dcd-59cf-54d2-80e6-c0d72b6bafdd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the LU Factorization Using Two or More Elementary Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a63866ed-9b40-5485-9cd8-7a78f3808192" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117205} {:answer/id #uuid "af02c1c5-96de-5a22-b251-3af3077eeb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117206} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4fa98c-66ce-5d3f-86bd-a8af33f35e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117207} {:answer/id #uuid "436d14b5-9dad-56b9-a8c7-7e1917829544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117208} {:answer/id #uuid "51476948-422f-5d01-acc3-5da96a178782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb4fa98c-66ce-5d3f-86bd-a8af33f35e